<div class="about-page">

  <section class="about-hero">
    <div class="about-label">About</div>
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="about-sub">Penumbra Forge LLC is a one-person software studio started in 2026. I maintain developer and security utilities and a few larger projects with explicit network boundaries. Most projects are still early — each one says where it stands.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="about-section">
    <h2>What I build</h2>
    <p>The common thread is local-first work with explicit network boundaries. Three areas:</p>
    <div class="about-grid">
      <div class="about-card">
        <h3>Local AI</h3>
        <p><a href="/umbra/">Umbra</a> is a private, unreleased local AI application being developed on Apple Silicon with MLX. A working prototype exists, but there is no public build or supported release yet. Site AI features can use WebGPU in the browser or an OpenAI-compatible endpoint you configure; that endpoint may be local or hosted.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="about-card">
        <h3>Security software</h3>
        <p><a href="/gate/">Gate</a> looks for secret-like patterns in source files and git history and can install a repository-local pre-commit hook. <a href="/vexes/">vexes</a> combines deterministic OSV advisory matches with heuristic evidence. <a href="/librarian/">mcp-librarian</a> gives coding agents a local skill library with recorded signature status. All three are experimental; heuristic results can produce false positives and miss things.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="about-card">
        <h3>Browser tools</h3>
        <p><a href="/tools/">{{ stats.liveTools }} mostly small browser-delivered utilities</a> for security work, encoding, and development. Many run entirely in the page; edge, network, and selectable-AI routes say where input goes. A subset of {{ stats.mcpTools }} deterministic operations is also <a href="/tools/agents/">exposed through MCP</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="about-section">
    <h2>Founder</h2>
    <div class="about-founder">
      <div class="about-founder-body">
        <h3>Shadoe Myers</h3>
        <p class="about-founder-role">Founder</p>
        <p>I'm a security engineer on a corporate incident-response team, where I build automation and internal tools. Penumbra Forge came out of a preference for tools that keep data on your own machine when their job permits it and clearly label the network when it does not. The projects here are my attempt at that, and they are still works in progress.</p>
        <div class="about-founder-links">
          <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/shadoe-myers" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/penumbraforge" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>
          <a href="mailto:shadoe@penumbraforge.com">shadoe@penumbraforge.com</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="about-section">
    <h2>How I work</h2>
    <ul class="about-principles">
      <li><strong>Local by default.</strong> Most tools do their work in the page. The ones that need the network are labelled, so you know before pasting anything in.</li>
      <li><strong>Prefer platform APIs.</strong> Use browser and runtime primitives where practical, while documenting the dependencies and upstream services that remain.</li>
      <li><strong>Open to inspection.</strong> The website repository is source-visible but not open-source licensed. Gate, vexes, and mcp-librarian publish separate open-source licenses. No accounts; analytics are limited to aggregate traffic measurement.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="about-section about-contact">
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Email <a href="mailto:shadoe@penumbraforge.com">shadoe@penumbraforge.com</a> — general, privacy, and security reports alike (see <a href="/.well-known/security.txt">security.txt</a>).</p>
  </section>

